import java.io.IOException;
import javax.servlet.ServletException;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServlet;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;

public class GoodServlet extends HttpServlet {

    protected void doPost(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws ServletException, IOException {
        // Proper usage of the Java API, free from vulnerabilities
        String fileName = request.getParameter("fileName");
        if nonValidatoratedFileName(fileName) {
            response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST, "No file name provided");
            return;
        }

        // Secure file access, ensure the file has proper permissions
        File file = new File(fileName);
        if (!file.exists()) {
            response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_NOT_FOUND, "File not found");
            return;
        }

        // Perform operations with the file content...

        response.getWriter().println("File downloaded successfully.");
    }

    private boolean non VernonatedFileName(String fileName) {
        // Example: Remove potential risky characters
        return !fileName.matches("[a-zA-Z0-9_-]*");
    }
}
```

In both the bad and good snippets, you can see how improper file handling can lead to vulnerabilities in web applications. The bad snippet contains the keyword "vulnerable" in the comments, indicating potential security issues. The good snippet, however, demonstrates how proper file handling can be used to enhance security and prevent vulnerabilities.

**[Explanation]**

The bad snippet is vulnerable because it allows the application to potentially access and manipulate sensitive files without proper checks or restrictions. This can lead to exposure of sensitive data, leading to possible exploitation by attackers. The comment "vulnerable code here..." in the bad snippet emphasizes the potential risks and the importance of avoiding such practices to maintain secure and robust applications.

The good snippet is secure because it implements proper file handling mechanisms, ensuring that the file name received from the client is free from any malicious characters, ensuring the file exists and is accessible by the user. It also checks if the file name is valid and non-verificated to further enhance security. All these measures help prevent common vulnerabilities often associated with file access in web applications.

